  "text": "Since the circles with diameters $AP$, $BQ$, and $CR$ intersect at points $X$, $Y$, and $Z$, we can use the fact that the circumcircles of $\\triangle PQR$ and $\\triangle ABQ$, $\\triangle CAQ$, and $\\triangle ABR$ also intersect at $X$, $Y$, and $Z$. This means that $\\triangle XYZ$ is the triangle formed by the midpoints of the sides of $\\triangle ABC$, which is the medial triangle of $\\triangle ABC$.\n\nIn a triangle, the medial triangle (formed by the midpoints of the sides) is similar to the original triangle with a ratio of similarity equal to $\\frac{1}{2}$. The area of the medial triangle is $\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{4}$ of the area of the original triangle.\n\nThe area of $\\triangle ABC$ can be found using Heron's formula. The semiperimeter $s$ of $\\triangle ABC$ is $\\frac{AB + BC + CA}{2} = \\frac{13 + 14 + 15}{2} = 21$. Therefore, the area $A$ of $\\triangle ABC$ is:\n\n$$A = \\sqrt{s(s - AB)(s - BC)(s - CA)} = \\sqrt{21(21 - 13)(21 - 14)(21 - 15)}$$\n$$= \\sqrt{21 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6} = \\sqrt{7056} = 84$$\n\nThe area of $\\triangle XYZ$ is thus $\\frac{1}{4}$ of the area of $\\triangle ABC$, which is $\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 84 = \\boxed{21}$.",
